flow "Consistent hashing"
summary "Maps keys and nodes onto a hash ring so joins and leaves remap only a small fraction of keys, using virtual nodes to avoid hot spots."
tags partitioning load-balancing caching
level intermediate
source "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consistent_hashing"

node router "Hash Router" service tone=primary
group ring "Hash ring (0..359 deg)"
node n-a "Node A (0 deg)" db in=ring
node n-b "Node B (120 deg)" db in=ring
node n-c "Node C (240 deg)" db in=ring
node n-d "Node D (180 deg)" db in=ring hidden
edge router > n-a
edge router > n-b
edge router > n-c
edge router > n-d hidden
array owners "Key -> clockwise owner" keys=k30,k150,k200,k300 = Node-B Node-C Node-C Node-A

scenario join-leave "Route, join, and leave on the ring"
  step route "Keys walk clockwise to next node"
    "Each key hashes to an angle on the ring and routes clockwise to the first node at or after it: `k30 -> Node B`, `k150` and `k200 -> Node C`, `k300 -> Node A`."
    detail "Routers store node ring positions in a sorted array or balanced binary search tree and binary-search for the smallest position greater than or equal to `hash(key)` in `O(log N)` time, wrapping around to the first node on the ring if `hash(key)` exceeds the largest node position."
    state router active
    send router > n-b "k30"
    send router > n-c "k150, k200" with
    send router > n-a "k300" with
    state n-a,n-b,n-c success
  step join "Node D joins at 180 deg"
    "Adding Node D splits only Node C's arc `(120, 240]`. Only `k150` migrates from C to D; every other key stays right where it is."
    show n-d,router-n-d
    state n-d active
    label owners#k150 "Node-D"
    state owners#k150 warning with
    send router > n-d "k150"
    callout n-d "Only 1 of 4 keys moved"
  step leave "Node B fails and leaves"
    "When Node B dies, only keys in its arc (`k30`) walk clockwise to the next live node (Node D). Roughly `1/N` of keys move instead of nearly all keys under `hash(k) % N`."
    state n-b error
    effect n-b shake
    label owners#k30 "Node-D"
    state owners#k30 warning with
    send router > n-d "k30"
    callout n-c "Node A and C are unaffected"

scenario hot-node "Hot node solved with virtual nodes" from=join-leave/route
  step skew "Uneven placement creates a hot node"
    "With few physical servers, random hash positions leave Node C owning a huge arc. Traffic piles onto Node C while Node A sits lightly loaded."
    state n-c error
    effect n-c shake
    label n-c "Node C (60% load)" with
    label n-a "Node A (15% load)" with
    callout n-c "Hot spot from skewed arc"
  step vnodes "Virtual nodes balance the ring"
    "Giving each physical node multiple virtual positions (`A#1..A#100`, `B#1..B#100`, `C#1..C#100`) splits the ring into many small arcs, evening out load and spreading failover across all survivors."
    detail "Virtual nodes reduce key-distribution variance and also handle heterogeneous hardware: a machine with twice the capacity receives twice as many virtual positions. When a physical server leaves, its scattered small arcs shift to many different survivors rather than overloading one clockwise neighbor."
    label n-a "Node A (33% · 100 vnodes)"
    label n-b "Node B (33% · 100 vnodes)" with
    label n-c "Node C (33% · 100 vnodes)" with
    state n-a,n-b,n-c success
    label owners#k200 "Node-A" with
    callout router "Smaller arcs = balanced load"
